1) Let's draw an arbitrary line $y=k-b$, intersecting the parabola at points $A$ and $B$. Then the abscissas of the endpoints of the chord $A$ and $B$ must satisfy the conditions: $k x - b = x^2$, i.e., $x^2 - k x + b = 0$, $x_A = \frac{k - \sqrt{k^2 - 4b}}{2}$, $x_B = \frac{k + \sqrt{k^2 - 4b}}{2}$. Then the abscissa $x_C$ of the midpoint of segment $AB$ is $x_C = \frac{x_A + x_B}{2} = \frac{k}{2}$ and depends only on the slope $k$, but not on $b$. Therefore, if two chords $A_1 B_1$ and $A_2 B_2$ are parallel, then their midpoints $C_1$ and $C_2$ have the same abscissas and the line $C_1 C_2$ is parallel to the $OY$ axis. A chord $A_0 B_0$ perpendicular to this line is parallel to the $OX$ axis and, due to symmetry, its midpoint lies on the $OY$ axis. Therefore, the line through point $C_0$ parallel to $C_1 C_2$ is the $OY$ axis. It intersects the parabola at the vertex $O$, and the line passing through point $O$ perpendicular to $OY$ is the $OX$ axis.
